{"type":"standard","title":"Linear complex structure","displaytitle":"<span lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><span class=\"mw-page-title-main\">Linear complex structure</span></span>","namespace":{"id":0,"text":""},"wikibase_item":"Q6553426","titles":{"canonical":"Linear_complex_structure","normalized":"Linear complex structure","display":"<span lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><span class=\"mw-page-title-main\">Linear complex structure</span></span>"},"pageid":1549805,"lang":"en","dir":"ltr","revision":"1365476629","tid":"a5c89ec3-85f7-11f1-97b3-4a3841d679b8","timestamp":"2026-07-22T18:03:31Z","description":"Mathematics concept","description_source":"local","content_urls":{"desktop":{"page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Linear_complex_structure","revisions":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Linear_complex_structure?action=history","edit":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Linear_complex_structure?action=edit","talk":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talk:Linear_complex_structure"},"mobile":{"page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Linear_complex_structure","revisions":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:History/Linear_complex_structure","edit":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Linear_complex_structure?action=edit","talk":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talk:Linear_complex_structure"}},"extract":"In mathematics, a complex structure on a real vector space  is an automorphism of  that squares to the minus identity, . Such a structure on  allows one to define multiplication by complex scalars in a canonical fashion so as to regard  as a complex vector space.","extract_html":"<p>In mathematics, a complex structure on a real vector space <span class=\"mwe-math-element mwe-math-element-inline\"><img src=\"https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/af0f6064540e84211d0ffe4dac72098adfa52845\" class=\"mwe-math-fallback-image-inline mw-invert skin-invert\" aria-hidden=\"true\" style=\"vertical-align:-0.338ex;width:1.787ex;height:2.176ex\" /></span> is an automorphism of <span class=\"mwe-math-element mwe-math-element-inline\"><img src=\"https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/af0f6064540e84211d0ffe4dac72098adfa52845\" class=\"mwe-math-fallback-image-inline mw-invert skin-invert\" aria-hidden=\"true\" style=\"vertical-align:-0.338ex;width:1.787ex;height:2.176ex\" /></span> that squares to the minus identity, <span class=\"mwe-math-element mwe-math-element-inline\"><img src=\"https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/8ba1faacb109617426b69062f2e111067003ed21\" class=\"mwe-math-fallback-image-inline mw-invert skin-invert\" aria-hidden=\"true\" style=\"vertical-align:-0.671ex;width:5.244ex;height:2.509ex\" /></span>. Such a structure on <span class=\"mwe-math-element mwe-math-element-inline\"><img src=\"https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/af0f6064540e84211d0ffe4dac72098adfa52845\" class=\"mwe-math-fallback-image-inline mw-invert skin-invert\" aria-hidden=\"true\" style=\"vertical-align:-0.338ex;width:1.787ex;height:2.176ex\" /></span> allows one to define multiplication by complex scalars in a canonical fashion so as to regard <span class=\"mwe-math-element mwe-math-element-inline\"><img src=\"https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/af0f6064540e84211d0ffe4dac72098adfa52845\" class=\"mwe-math-fallback-image-inline mw-invert skin-invert\" aria-hidden=\"true\" style=\"vertical-align:-0.338ex;width:1.787ex;height:2.176ex\" /></span> as a complex vector space.</p>"}